---
import ProductLayout from "../layouts/ProductLayout.astro";
import Shot from "../components/Shot.astro";

const description = "How Nostekon grades restore evidence, from V0 backup status to V4 data correctness, plus recovery time, exact data loss and the limits of what a report can prove.";

const levels = [
  ["V0", "Backup reported success", "The backup tool says it finished, and names the backup and its archived WAL."],
  ["V1", "Restore reported success", "The restore job completed and a recovery point was selected."],
  ["V2", "Workload is healthy", "Pods are ready and HTTP and TCP health checks answer."],
  ["V3", "Data is structurally intact", "Tables exist, row counts match and checksums agree."],
  ["V4", "Application data is correct", "Business rules hold, every acknowledged write is accounted for, and stores agree with each other."],
];
---
<ProductLayout title="Evidence" description={description} current="evidence">
  <section class="page-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>How deep did the recovery actually go?</h1>
      <p class="lead">Five ordered levels make restore claims comparable. A report gives the deepest level that passed in order, and the first check that failed. Passing V0 says nothing about V1 to V4.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="levels">
      {levels.map(([id, title, text]) => (
        <div class="level">
          <span class="level-id">{id}</span>
          <h3>{title}</h3>
          <p>{text}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="prose" style="margin-top: 20px;"><a class="link" href="/docs/levels/">Read the full verification reference</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="margin-top: 40px;">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>A failed drill, read in one screen</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">The report keeps the reason next to the verdict, so nobody has to dig through logs to learn what went wrong.</p>
      </div>
      <Shot name="report-failed" height={900} alt="Nostekon Studio report for a synthetic cluster-loss drill that failed at V3: row counts did not match, and recovery took longer than the objective." caption="Synthetic sample evidence, evaluated by the Go engine in your browser. It is not a recorded restore." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head"><h2>A valid signature is not a successful recovery</h2><p class="section-lead">Keep byte integrity, key trust and recovery results separate.</p></div>
      <dl class="facts">
        <div><dt>Authenticate the public key</dt><dd>Studio imports only Ed25519 public keys. Compare the full fingerprint with the owner through a trusted channel before explicitly granting local trust. A key beside an evidence file is not automatically trustworthy.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Verify the original bytes</dt><dd>The same Go verifier runs in the API, CLI and browser. Even an extra newline changes the digest. A successful signature check enables exact original-file export, not a new recovery verdict.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Revoke without erasing</dt><dd>Remove local trust when a key should no longer be used. Verification receipts are cleared, while original evidence remains saved. Browser policy does not change the server's separately configured trusted keys.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="prose" style="margin: 24px 0 40px;"><a class="link" href="/docs/reference/drillrun-evidence/#verify-in-studio-or-the-browser-demo">Verify signatures and recheck exported originals</a></p>
      <div class="split">
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>Recovery time</h3>
          <p>Measured from the moment the failure was injected to the moment recovery finished. Each phase is plotted on that same clock, so time that falls between phases stays visible instead of disappearing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="prose">
          <h3>Data loss</h3>
          <p>The ledger lists every write that was acknowledged before the failure. After recovery, Nostekon checks which of those IDs came back. The loss window runs from the last recovered write to the failure, and a missing write in the middle is reported as a gap.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <h3>A report judges the evidence it is given</h3>
        <p>A DrillRun file holds recorded check results and write IDs. Until a trusted orchestrator collects those observations itself, the report is only as honest as whoever recorded them. A signature proves the file was not changed afterwards; it does not prove the observations were true. Reports are not compliance certificates.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProductLayout>
